<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Veliko Tarnovo</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tarnovo, Trnovo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Veliko Tarnovo is a city in north-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> on the Yantra river, built on hills including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsarevets">Tsarevets</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trapezitsa">Trapezitsa</a>. As Tarnovo it was the capital of the Second Bulgarian Empire from the Asen revolt of 1185 until its capture by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in 1393, and a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> art and learning. In 1879 the assembly of the new Principality of Bulgaria adopted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarnovo-constitution">Tarnovo Constitution</a> there, and in 1908 Bulgaria's full independence was proclaimed in the city.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/veliko-tarnovo.jpg" alt="Veliko Tarnovo" width="320" height="472" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: File:Tsarevets-Panorama.jpg, www.vacacionesbulgaria.com (Infobgv); File:Church of SS. Peter and Paul, Tarnovo.JPG, Kandi … · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Collage_of_views_of_VT_G.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The old part of Veliko Tarnovo is built on three hills, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsarevets">Tsarevets</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trapezitsa">Trapezitsa</a> and Sveta Gora, rising amid the meanders of the Yantra River. On Tsarevets stood the palaces of the Bulgarian emperors, the Patriarchate and the Patriarchal Cathedral, surrounded by thick walls. Trapezitsa is known for its many churches and as the former main residence of the nobility. The town has always held a strategic position on main roads connecting the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a>. Its names Tarnovgrad and Tarnovo most probably come from the Old Bulgarian word for thorny, and the word veliko, meaning great, was added in 1965 in honour of its status as an old capital.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Veliko Tarnovo is one of the oldest settlements in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, with a history of more than five millennia; the first traces of human presence, from the 3rd millennium BCE, were found on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trapezitsa">Trapezitsa</a>. It was a stronghold of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire</a>, with an important military garrison. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uprising-of-asen-and-peter">Uprising of Asen and Peter</a> began on 26 October 1185, the feast day of St. Demetrius of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessaloniki</a>, and ended with the restoration of Bulgaria as the Second Bulgarian Empire, ruled by the Asen dynasty, with Tarnovo as capital.</p><p>From the 12th to the 14th century Tarnovgrad was the strongest Bulgarian fortification and its most prosperous city, and the political, economic, cultural and religious centre of the empire. It covered about 70 hectares across the hills. The Bulgarian cleric <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregory-tsamblak">Gregory Tsamblak</a> described it in the 14th century as a very large, handsome city surrounded by walls, with 12,000 to 15,000 inhabitants. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> captured it on 17 July 1393 after a three-month siege in which the Bulgarian Patriarch Evtimiy led the defence, and three years later conquered the entire Bulgarian Empire.</p><p>On 7 July 1877 the Russian general Joseph Vladimirovich Gourko liberated the city, ending 480 years of Ottoman rule. In 1878 the Treaty of Berlin created a Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkan-mountains">Stara Planina</a> range, with its seat at Veliko Tarnovo. In the early 1950s, under Communist rule, the city underwent intensive urbanisation, expanding to the west.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Veliko Tarnovo is the administrative centre of Veliko Tarnovo Province in north central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, often called the City of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsar">Tsars</a>. It had 68,783 inhabitants at the 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. It is the seat of Veliko Tarnovo University, and its monuments include the Monument to the Asen Dynasty, built in 1985.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Veliko Tarnovo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At the Battle of Adrianople on 14 April 1205 the Bulgarian tsar Kaloyan and his Cuman allies crushed the Latin heavy cavalry and captured Emperor Baldwin, who died in prison in <b>Tarnovo</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/latin-empire">Latin Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After his capture at Adrianople in 1205, the Latin emperor Baldwin I was held at <b>Tarnovo</b>; a tower of the Tsarevets fortress is still known as Baldwin's Tower.</blockquote><a href="/empire/second-bulgarian-empire">Second Bulgarian Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>On 5 October 1908, during the crisis over Bosnia, Ferdinand proclaimed full independence at <b>Tarnovo</b> and took the title of tsar, creating the Kingdom of Bulgaria.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-bulgaria">Principality of Bulgaria</a></li><li><blockquote>Sratsimir ruled from Vidin's Baba Vida fortress over lands along the Danube, minting his own coins and placing his church under the Patriarch of Constantinople rather than <b>Tarnovo</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/tsardom-of-vidin">Tsardom of Vidin</a></li></ul></section>
